WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:04.140
Self-driving cars are already here.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.860
Widespread adoption of this technology will lead

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:11.700
to less traffic, less pollution,
and fewer car accidents.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.320
These cars bring to life a
longstanding hypothetical dilemma -

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.660
how moral should we make our robots?

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.820
What if an autonomous vehicle
is faced with the choice

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.200
to either kill its passengers or kill others?

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.869
How should it be programmed?

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:31.769
In online surveys, participants approve of
autonomous vehicles

00:00:31.769 --> 00:00:35.529
that sacrifice the driver to save many others

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.690
Basically a utilitarian approach that aims

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:40.600
to preserve the most amount of life

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:45.300
But respondents would prefer
not to buy these kinds of cars.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.980
And, they don’t want regulations that

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.080
require self-sacrificing algorithms.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.860
In fact, such regulations would make them

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.880
less willing to buy a self-driving car.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.160
The rise of autonomous vehicles 
creates a social dilemma:

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.460
Roads would be much safer

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.699
if there were more autonomous cars.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.760
But programming that would
actually make driving safer,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.880
might prevent people from
buying these vehicles

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.770
Imagine shopping for a car and having the choice -

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.520
a car that sacrifices the driver on occasion

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.720
or a “preserve the passenger
at all costs” car.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.720
What would you do?

